+### Proposed session bootstrap and coordination — UNPROVEN
+
+Discovery precedes normal route capture, so an origin-bearing page is needed before same-page window.fetch; fetch from about:blank must not be assumed to bypass CORS. **Proposal for review:** one explicit browser navigation to the supplied in-scope site URL seeds the origin and stores a discovery bootstrap document. Count it as an intentional document operation and within the smoke’s ten distinct document-capture slots. If it is a discovered selected route, reuse its saved HTML/outcome, never navigate it twice. If it is not in the discovered set, retain it as discovery source without an extra pages[] outcome and allow at most nine later route captures for the smoke, reporting that distinction. Full runs similarly reuse it where mapped. No root REST bootstrap. Additional origin bootstrap is **part of the proposed plan/bounds, not a live request now**. Test CORS/origin/session assumptions locally; inability to read in the established session is failure, not permission for a different client or security change.
+
+After controlled bootstrap, intentional robots/sitemaps/declared REST collections and permitted HEAD use the owned browser page. Planned fetch may use browser redirect handling only while each request-stage redirect is held by CDP and approved; fetch redirect:error by itself cannot satisfy permitted-hop coverage. Readback does not claim that redirect-aware replacement for the historical redirect:error diagnostic already works. Apex/www cross-origin reads may still fail CORS; retain typed failure with transport truth, no alternate-route retry.
+
+One monotonic coordinator serializes intentional operations/hops, tracks armed URL/method/frame plus CDP request/network/redirect identity, completion time, request/byte/deadline budgets and sticky stop. A background request matching an armed URL ambiguously must fail the control proof, not be silently counted as intentional. Do not inject identity/operation headers or cookies. Before each intentional dispatch, check scheme/host/no credentials, redirect cycle/hops≤5, remaining budget, monotonic deadline and completion+5 s. Recheck after waits. Collection operation completion is terminal body transfer/read (or definite terminal failure), and for rendered capture also the capture/save result; resources loading in parallel are not individually delayed. Same gap shared across streams and bootstrap. No second attempt after failure. A terminal refusal stops admission immediately; preserve available bodies/metadata, close browser, report in-flight resource events and mark future deliberate work skipped.
+
+Session/controller interception of redirect chains, SW targets, bootstrap identity, byte ceilings, session lifetime, true response-completion ordering and event races are **unproven implementation mechanisms**. They are hard local-fixture gates before CHK/live, not covered by the historical one-page fetch. On unsupported interception/correlation: stop/finalize with an explicit control failure; no browser security change, fetch client fallback or invented successful response.
