+## 3. Soft-block signals and local control proof
+
+Inspected `C4/antibot_detector.py:191–281`: `is_blocked(status_code, html, error_message=None)` returns `(bool, reason)`. CrawlResult has success/error_message/crawl_stats, **no dedicated anti-bot flag** (`models.py:130–168`). `async_webcrawler.py:630–634` sets success False/error_message “Blocked by anti-bot protection: …”; :512–519 records per-attempt blocked/reason in crawl_stats. Existing product checks status 403/429 first but otherwise classifies success=False as generic failed; it does not map all explicit soft challenges to blocked. Library heuristics include 200 near-empty content and structural shells; generic Tier-2 Access Denied is not universally applied to HTTP 200. Thus reading an assumed flag or every library error as refusal would be incorrect.
+
+**Planned explicit detector (E-19), not implemented:** intentional status 403/429 is refusal; cf-mitigated:challenge header is explicit; HTML main-document title normalized to `access denied`, `just a moment`, `attention required`, or `verify you are human`, together with a denial/challenge body or structural challenge form, is a declared predicate. The F-03 200 soft-denial body is specified with `<title>Access Denied</title>` and an explicit denial message, so detection is testable. Structural challenge-form/token or blocking challenge iframe is corroborated as document-preventing, not inferred from an unrelated CAPTCHA/script in an otherwise usable article. Record predicate, inspected source locator/header, status and available library reason. Historical wrapper used title/body checks (`...063951Z` wrapper inspect_dom); do not promote its broad string scan wholesale.
+
+Plain empty body remains unsupported/empty_body; a near-empty/structural-only library heuristic is not proof of challenge and must be reported as unusable/incomplete failure rather than successful content or automatic site refusal. Ordinary 404/5xx/network/timeouts remain typed failures with no retry. An unrelated third-party analytics refusal is incidental evidence, not requested-document denial. A real document-preventing challenge stops regardless of HTTP 200. No challenge-solving, clicks/fills/submits. New detection precision remains unproven, with both positive and false-positive fixture cases required.
+
+All future controls run against loopback only, using real installed Chromium/Crawl4AI and product adapter; no live targets, DNS escape or package install. A test-only egress guard prevents non-loopback dispatch, including F-05/F-07 external resource markup; denial log proves it, not merely a fixture server log that cannot observe external hosts. Do not treat a stubbed crawler as proof of real browser interception. Independent server logs plus browser events/timing provide both sides.
